"}],"key":"kLm3ZejMxw"},{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Normal if n ≥ 30 (always normal if the population is normal)","position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"key":"B514maGBwS"}],"key":"OCX4ZDHUAN"}],"key":"HlM5rkD6HI"}],"key":"V5DSOLDmpt"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Check Your Understanding: The Central Limit Theorem in Action","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"m01OS7xUUe"}],"identifier":"check-your-understanding-the-central-limit-theorem-in-action","label":"Check Your Understanding: The Central Limit Theorem in Action","html_id":"check-your-understanding-the-central-limit-theorem-in-action","implicit":true,"key":"vQ1euWyCN5"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Recall our earlier scenario: the average Pell grant award for 2007-2008 was $2,600, with a standard deviation of $500. Nothing was said about the shape of the distribution of individual awards.","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"key":"GjeRSqYxbU"}],"key":"lyXW808bqd"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-a12b495d51e99bfc7862c0d02cefdade.mjs","model":{"question":"Can we compute the probability that the mean award in a random sample of 8 recipients exceeds \\$2,800?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"No—the sample is too small and the population shape is unknown","feedback":"Correct! With a small sample (8) and an unknown (possibly non-normal) population shape, the CLT doesn't apply, so we can't do a normal calculation."},{"correct":false,"text":"Yes—sample means are always normal","feedback":"n = 8 is far below the rule of thumb of 30, and we don't know the population is normal."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Is the sample large enough for the Central Limit Theorem, and do we know the population's shape?","explanation":""},"key":"TsjnlnCHRK"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-a12b495d51e99bfc7862c0d02cefdade.mjs","model":{"question":"What about the probability that the mean award in a random sample of 100 recipients exceeds \\$2,700?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"Yes—about 0.025","feedback":"Correct! By the CLT, x-bar is approximately normal with mean 2,600 and SD 50; z = 2, so P ≈ 0.025."},{"correct":false,"text":"Yes—about 0.16","feedback":"0.16 corresponds to 1 standard deviation; \\$2,700 is 2 standard deviations above the mean here."},{"correct":false,"text":"No—we can't compute it","feedback":"The CLT applies because n = 100 is large, even if individual awards aren't normal."}],"multi":false,"hint":"n = 100 ≥ 30, so the CLT applies: SD of the mean = 500/√100 = 50, z = (2700 − 2600)/50 = 2.","explanation":""},"key":"gNuJqafneR"},{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example: Household Size","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"key":"fOIofPjGfS"}],"key":"BgL1jtIVqb"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":35,"column":1},"end":{"line":35,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Household size in the United States has a mean of 2.6 people and standard deviation of 1.4 people.","position":{"start":{"line":35,"column":1},"end":{"line":35,"column":1}},"key":"omLuMLMUgi"}],"key":"oVOQ1xpPOG"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":37,"column":1},"end":{"line":37,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"(a) What is the probability that a randomly chosen household has more than 3 people?","position":{"start":{"line":37,"column":1},"end":{"line":37,"column":1}},"key":"pRjj7MCXtH"}],"key":"fteaI6LtHe"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A normal approximation should not be used here, because the distribution of household sizes would be considerably skewed to the right. We do not have enough information to solve this problem.","position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"key":"oMSOQfEXnk"}],"key":"ZAZiQsa2g2"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"(b) What is the probability that the mean size of a random sample of 10 households is more than 3?","position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"key":"rHH9vV8huH"}],"key":"dVb11KSHJb"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"By anyone’s standards, 10 is a small sample size. The Central Limit Theorem does not guarantee a sample mean coming from a skewed population to be approximately normal unless the sample size is large.","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"key":"CY6H2WM3ED"}],"key":"bXa6gAWELJ"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"(c) What is the probability that the mean size of a random sample of 100 households is more than 3?","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"key":"pitv5FfNy2"}],"key":"RVNsbc4qWq"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":47,"column":1},"end":{"line":47,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Now we may invoke the Central Limit Theorem: even though the distribution of household size X is skewed, the distribution of sample mean household size ","position":{"start":{"line":47,"column":1},"end":{"line":47,"column":1}},"key":"h3cVoVYMcP"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"\\bar{X}","position":{"start":{"line":47,"column":1},"end":{"line":47,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"k